1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,700
Slide 6 reveals the technical rationale: BYPASSING THE BROWSER SANDBOX.

2
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:10,918
For years, web browsers were built as airtight security prisons.

3
00:00:10,918 --> 00:00:15,360
Why did Google break out of their own Chrome prison, James?

4
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:19,418
Because the browser sandbox has strict security handcuffs!

5
00:00:19,418 --> 00:00:31,340
Look at the left card: a website in Chrome cannot touch your local `C:\` drive, cannot capture your desktop screen without explicit permissions, and cannot listen for global system hotkeys!

6
00:00:31,740 --> 00:00:32,940
Exactly!

7
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:44,940
If you want an AI that can organize your local Downloads folder, inspect your VS Code editor, and summarize a PDF sitting on your desktop, you MUST have native OS shell privileges on the right card!

8
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:49,111
But with great OS privilege comes massive security danger!

9
00:00:49,111 --> 00:00:55,420
If a malicious prompt compromises a desktop agent, it has access to your entire local hard drive!

10
00:00:55,820 --> 00:01:02,080
That is why local-first architecture requires strict tenant boundaries and cryptographic audits.

